Consider a spherical Gaussian surface of radius $r$ centred on a spherically symmetric mass distribution, so that $\phi = \phi(r)$ depends only on the radial coordinate. By expressing the scalar area element in spherical coordinates as $dS = r^2\sin\theta\, d\theta\, d\varphi$ and evaluating the angular integrals, show that

$$\int_S \frac{\partial\phi}{\partial r}\, dS = 4\pi r^2 \frac{\partial\phi}{\partial r}$$

